²y²)+2abxy(bx+ay) = (bx+ay)(ax+by)². Dividing by bx+ay gives (ax+by)² on the original domain bx+ay ≠ 0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1988_AMC_12_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_156","cnm_157","cnm_148","cnm_150"],"id":"common-part-factorization","label":"Collect the shared terms","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-part-factorization":["cnm_158","cnm_151","cnm_153"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_158","cnm_151","cnm_153","cnm_156","cnm_157","cnm_148","cnm_150"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 2 - 2√2","reason":"The only possible parallel pair requires cos x+tan x=cot x+sin x. Writing c=cos x and s=sin x reduces this to (c-s)(cs-c-s)=0, so cs=c+s. Let u=c+s, which is negative on the stated interval. Then u^2=1+2cs=1+2u, giving u=1-√2. Therefore sin(2x)=2cs=2-2√2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_12B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"parabolic-chord-slopes-and-identities","label":"Compare chord slopes and reduce the trigonometric equation","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parabolic-chord-slopes-and-identities":["cnm_h167","math:reciprocal-trig-identities","cnm_h062","cnm_h061","cnm_153","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h167","math:reciprocal-trig-identities","cnm_h062","cnm_h061","cnm_153","cnm_220"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. b","reason":"The logarithms require x>0. With t=log_b(x)≠0, the equation becomes t/2+1/(2t)=1, hence (t-1)^2=0. Thus t=1 and x=b.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1964_AMC_12_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h043","cnm_h046","cnm_156","cnm_153"],"id":"common-logarithm-base","label":"Express both logarithms in one base","methodDir